          %h3#standard_definition Standard definition
          %p
            A
            %strong simple group
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            with exactly two
            = succeed "s:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+group"} quotient group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+quotient+group"} trivial quotient group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{1\} \cong G/G
            and the group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \cong G/\{1\}
            itself.
          %p
            Equivalently, a simple group is a group possessing exactly two
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+subgroup"} normal subgroups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+subgroup"} trivial subgroup
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{1\}
            and the group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            itself. One can also say that a normal subgroup is trivial iff it is not
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            Note that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+group"} trivial group
            does not itself count as simple, on the grounds that it has only
            %em one
            quotient group (or only one normal subgroup). It may be possible to find authors that use “at most” in place of “exactly”, thereby allowing the trivial group to be simple. (Compare
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/too+simple+to+be+simple"} too simple to be simple
          %h3#in_constructive_algebra In constructive algebra
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tight+apartness"} tight apartness
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≠
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ne
            such that the group operations are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strongly+extensional+function"} strongly extensional
            and use the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antisubgroups"} antisubgroups
            (which classically are the complements of subgroups). Then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is
            %strong simple
            if, given any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+antisubgroup"} normal antisubgroup
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            is trivial iff it is proper. Explicitly, this says
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            owns every nonidentity element (every
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            such that
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \ne 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+subset"} inhabited
            (with some
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            such that necessarily
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \ne 1
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#finite_simple_groups Finite simple groups
          %p
            Simple groups are most commonly encountered in the theory of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+group"} finite group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            admits a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition+series"} composition series
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ⊆
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi …
                  %mo ⊆
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 = G_0 \subseteq G_1 \subseteq \ldots \subseteq G_n = G
          %p
            where each inclusion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi i
                  %mo ⊆
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_i \subseteq G_{i+1}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+subgroup"} normal subgroup
            and the quotient
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_{i+1}/G_i
            (called a
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong composition factor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan-H%C3%B6lder+theorem"} Jordan-Hölder theorem
            ensures that any two composition series have the same length and the same composition factors (up to permutation).
          %p
            Thus finite simple groups are in some sense the primitive building blocks of finite groups generally. The massive program of classifying all finite simple groups was announced as completed by Daniel Gorenstein in 1983, although some doubts remained because there were some gaps in proofs. Most if not all the gaps are considered by experts in the area to have been filled, but there remain some notable skeptics, including for example
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Pierre+Serre"} Jean-Pierre Serre
          %blockquote
            %p
              Whenever I asked the specialists, they replied something like: “Oh no, it is not a gap; it is just something which has not been written, but there is an incomplete unpublished 800-page manuscript on it.” For me, it was just the same as a “gap,” and I could not understand why it was not acknowledged as such. (
              %a{:href => "https://cs.nyu.edu/pipermail/fom/2008-November/013181.html"}> Source
              )
          %p
            and possibly also
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+H.+Conway"} John H. Conway
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://cs.nyu.edu/pipermail/fom/2008-November/013178.html"} Joe Shipman
          %blockquote
            %p A few months ago I was discussing the COFSG with John Conway and he was still pessimistic. (Meaning of course that he was confident the classification was correct, “optimistic” in his case means there are previously undiscovered finite simple groups, because they’re beautiful and interesting objects and it would be disappointing to have no more.)
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classification+of+finite+simple+groups"} classification of finite simple groups
          %h3#directed_colimits Directed colimits
          #directed.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\alpha
              be a directed system of simple groups and monomorphisms between them. Then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi colim
                      %mi α
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} colim_\alpha S_\alpha
              is also simple.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              is a normal subgroup of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi colim
                      %mi α
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} colim_\alpha S_\alpha
              with a non-identity element. Then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi α
                    %mo =
                    %mi N
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_\alpha = N \cap S_\alpha
              is normal in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\alpha
              (if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in S_\alpha
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi N
                      %mo ∩
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in N \cap S_\alpha
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mi y
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x y x^{-1}
              belongs to both
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              and
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\alpha
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              is large enough that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\alpha
              contains a non-identity element of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\alpha
              that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi N
                        %mi α
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_\alpha = S_\alpha
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi α
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_\alpha = S_\alpha
              for all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \in S
              there is
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              such that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \in S_\alpha
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi α
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi N
                        %mi α
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \in S_\alpha = N_\alpha \subseteq N
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              contains any
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \in S
          %h3#infinite_simple_groups Infinite simple groups
          %p
            There are simple groups of any infinite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+group"} automorphism group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Aut
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi κ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Aut(\kappa)
            containing all 3-cycles (this is the infinite version of the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/alternating+group"} alternating group
          %p
            To see that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Alt
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi κ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Alt(\kappa)
            is simple, it is enough to observe that it is a directed colimit of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Alt
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Alt(X)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            ranges over finite subsets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            of cardinality at least
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 5
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 5
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_n
            for integers
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 0
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#directed"}
          %p
            A rather deeper set of examples is afforded by the following result, which follows from a theorem named after
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://groupprops.subwiki.org/wiki/Baer-Schreier-Ulam_theorem"} Baer, Ulam, and Schreier
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              For an infinite set
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sym
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sym(X)
              is contained in a maximal such normal subgroup
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi N
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|X|}
              many elements of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|X|}
              is the cardinality of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            It follows that the quotient group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mi Sym
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_X = Sym(X)/N_X
            is simple, and we have the following corollary.
          .num_cor
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p Every group embeds into a simple group.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              For finite groups
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              (WLOG, of cardinality at least
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Sym
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \hookrightarrow Sym(G)
              into the permutation group of the underlying set, and there is an embedding
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sym
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Alt
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sym(G) \hookrightarrow Alt(G + 2)
              which carries an even permutation on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              to the obvious even permutation on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G + 2
              that fixes the elements
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              to the even permutation
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi (a\; b)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              embeds in a simple group
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Alt
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi G
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Alt(G + 2)
            %p
              An infinite group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              embeds in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_G
              via the Cayley embedding
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Sym
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Sym
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi G
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \hookrightarrow Sym(G) \to Sym(G)/N_G = Q_G
            %p
              noting that for any non-identity
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \in G
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cayley
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi h
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi g
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cayley(g) = (h \mapsto g h)
              has no fixed points, hence does not belong to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi N
                        %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_G
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \to Q_G
              is indeed monic.
          %h4#tarski_monsters Tarski monsters
          %p
            Among the infinite simple groups, there are curious examples called “Tarski monsters” for primes
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo &gt;
                    %msup
                      %mn 10
                      %mn 75
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \gt 10^{75}
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarski_monster_group"} Wikipedia
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo &gt;
                  %msup
                    %mn 10
                    %mn 75
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \gt 10^{75}
            there are continuum many monsters for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            that are distinct up to isomorphism.
